{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790540458,"nonce":"fDfjZYvVcG9Vf1J4vTxv+aknXzQ=","message":"Done in a4bcddf07497372cd7447341760a4d8ac9915d8b, with Ash e0bd482 pinned in 3c64b9305bfa8d2600350e05b713c631f9604f71. caribou's scheduler takes fibers from the host where krio cannot switch stacks (sched::host_fiber); a task that may suspend runs on one, and the host's suspension is krio's suspender, taken only on a host fiber. caribou-runtime lends Ash's ash_wasm_runtime::guest::Fiber, registered with the heap as a fiber stack; the driver sets AotRequest::wasm_fibers for every wasm build. Checked: a two-thread ping-pong over sys.thread.Lock prints 'ping 0, pong 0, ping 1, pong 1, ping 2, pong 2' under caribou run on wasm32-wasip1 and wasm32-wasip1-threads and in Chrome (before: 'pong 0, pong 1, pong 2, ping 0, ...', the waits falling through). Threads still run in parallel (8e7 iterations: 1 thread 149 ms, 2 77, 4 41; about 20% over the uninstrumented build). The flock example runs in Chrome with the transform (module 8.9 MB to 12.7 MB). Not covered here: the scheduler's integration tests on wasm (05d4384); Wren's own Fiber in compiled Wren is WrenLift's (comment #4).","files":null}]}